{"id": "sdqa0100", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 4.4, "n_chunks": 5, "n_concurrent": 5, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.114, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "exerts control by switching heating or cooling devices on or off, or by regulating the flow of a heat transfer fluid as needed, to maintain the correct temperature", "query": "How do thermostats work?", "transcript": "How do thermostats work?", "mode": "local", "answer": "A thermostat is a device that controls the temperature in a building by turning heating or cooling systems on and off. It works by sensing the current temperature and comparing it to the desired temperature setting. When the actual temperature is lower than the set point, the thermostat sends a signal to activate the heating system. Once the room reaches the desired temperature, the thermostat turns off the heater. Conversely, if the temperature is higher than the set point, the thermostat activates the cooling system until the desired temperature is reached, then turns it off. Modern thermostats can also learn your schedule and preferences to optimize comfort and energy use.", "answer_ms": 2740}
